  <static seed="21">
    <statement><p>Find the derivative of <m>1-e^{-(x/L)^k}\text{,}</m> where <m>L</m> and <m>k</m> are constants with <m>L&gt;0</m> and <m>k&gt;1\text{.}</m></p>
<p><m>f'(x)=</m> <fillin characters="50" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{kx^{k-1}\exp\mathopen{}\left(-\left(\frac{x}{L}\right)^{k}\right)}{L^{k}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>Using the chain rule,</p>
<p><me>\begin{aligned}
          \frac{d}{dx}\left(1-e^{-(x/L)^k}\right)
          \amp = -e^{-(x/L)^k}\left(-k(x/L)^{k-1}\frac{1}{L}\right)\\
          \amp = \frac{k}{L}(x/L)^{k-1}e^{-(x/L)^k}.
          \end{aligned}</me></p>
</solution>
